WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.368
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.991 --> 00:00:09.740
EVAN BROWN: Hello.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.850
I'm Evan, and I'm an
engineer at Google.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:12.683
DAN WENDORF: And hi.

00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:14.017
I'm Dan, an engineer at Pivotal.

00:00:14.017 --> 00:00:16.391
EVAN BROWN: Google and Pivotal
have been working together

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:17.610
the better part of this year.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.450
DAN WENDORF: Sorry, Evan.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.830
It's the best part of this year.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.696
EVAN BROWN: I agree.

00:00:20.696 --> 00:00:23.070
We've been working to bring
Cloud Foundry to Google Cloud

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:23.880
Platform.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.220
And a lot of folks on
both sides were involved.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.679
Today, Dan and I are going to
talk about some of that work.

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:30.886
DAN WENDORF: And we'll have
more videos in the coming

00:00:30.886 --> 00:00:33.000
weeks featuring other
engineers from both Pivotal

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.150
and Google talking in depth
about service brokers, logging,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.140
and monitoring.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.380
But for today, it's me and
you talking to the internet

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.450
about BOSH and Cloud Foundry.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.580
Evan, you picked up some
work earlier this year

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.561
to bring BOSH to GCP.

00:00:46.561 --> 00:00:48.310
Can you talk a little
bit more about that?

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.018
EVAN BROWN: Sure.

00:00:49.018 --> 00:00:51.322
BOSH is a lot of things,
but our initial focus

00:00:51.322 --> 00:00:53.280
was really simple, and
that was to make it talk

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.785
to Google's infrastructure.

00:00:54.785 --> 00:00:56.160
We made that work
pretty quickly.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.740
We achieved that goal, and
then we set loftier goals.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.260
We wanted Google to
be the best place

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.390
for BOSH to deploy
applications to,

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.489
especially things
like Cloud Foundry.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:08.730
So those lofty goals that we
set included adding support

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.050
for Google's global network,
their global load balancer,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.380
and 30-second VM boot
times, live migration,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.339
and preemptable VM pricing,
just to name a few.

00:01:18.339 --> 00:01:20.630
DAN WENDORF: And this work,
you did at all with Pivotal

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:21.630
and it's open source?

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.421
EVAN BROWN: That's
right, all with Pivotal.

00:01:23.421 --> 00:01:25.980
We spent a lot of time here
in San Francisco with Pivots

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:26.670
like yourself.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.640
The work that we've done has
been on GitHub since day one.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.500
I think we hit a lot
of those lofty goals.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.150
And I'd like to show
you by spinning up

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.640
a BOSH director on
GCP and setting it up

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.660
to deploy some software.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.530
DAN WENDORF: All
right, sounds great.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.830
EVAN BROWN: So I'm starting
inside the GCP console.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.980
And I've got access
to a terminal here.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.230
And the terminal, it's
called Cloud Shell.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.730
And it's basically
a free VM where

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.700
I can do things like
deploy the infrastructure

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.090
that BOSH needs to run.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.154
So we're using Terraform
to do this deployment.

00:01:54.154 --> 00:01:56.070
It's provisioning all
of the networking that's

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.810
required for BOSH to run.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.240
And we've got this handy
architecture diagram

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.560
that basically shows the bits
that are there for BOSH to run.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.380
The most important thing is that
we would have a BOSH director.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.269
And that BOSH director is going
to have a private IP address.

00:02:10.269 --> 00:02:12.060
So it's not sitting on
the public internet,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.610
but it is able to talk
to the public internet

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.260
through a NAT instance.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.640
To talk to the BOSH director
directly, we have a bastion VM.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.460
So you're able to SSH
into the bastion VM

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.110
or use SSH agent forwarding
to do things like BOSH target

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.520
and BOSH deploy.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.280
So we use Terraform to provision
all of this infrastructure.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.360
We do that from
within Cloud Shell.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.050
And once we're inside
our BOSH bastion VM,

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:42.730
we can use BOSH-init to stand
up that first BOSH director.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.440
This takes a little bit longer
than we're showing in the demo

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:45.940
here.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.070
We've sped it up for time
purposes, but at this point,

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:50.480
we've got that global network.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.190
We have our BOSH
director up and running.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.620
And we would be able
to deploy Cloud Foundry

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.460
or Concourse or any other
BOSHified application.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.002
And Dan, I know you work on
Cloud Foundry at Pivotal.

00:03:01.002 --> 00:03:02.960
And you did a lot of work
to make it run really

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.420
well on Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.040
So would you be able to talk
a little bit about that work?

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:07.940
DAN WENDORF: Sure.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.310
One of Cloud Foundry's
biggest selling points

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.780
is its ability to run great
and many cloud platforms,

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.250
abstracting away boring
details, while really exposing

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.960
what makes a platform unique.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.520
To get it running
great on Google,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.500
our engineers dove
into the infrastructure

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.660
to really understand
its ins and outs

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.520
and to figure out where
Cloud Foundry needed

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.290
to change to run perfectly.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.600
So we spent a lot
of time focusing

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.660
on networking configuration
to get the fast and solid

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.320
networking that
Google's known for

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.530
and bringing in other
services like Google Cloud

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.924
Storage for native blob stores.

00:03:38.924 --> 00:03:39.840
EVAN BROWN: Very cool.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.560
There's a link below where
you can follow a tutorial

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.410
to deploy open-source Cloud
Foundry on the Google Cloud

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.130
Platform.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.750
But Dan, I know there
are other ways to get up

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.350
and running with Cloud
Foundry on Google.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.690
Could you talk a
little bit about that?

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:51.814
DAN WENDORF: Yeah, totally.

00:03:51.814 --> 00:03:54.170
Cloud Foundry's a highly
customizable platform,

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.700
even down to how it's deployed.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.040
On our doc site at
docs.pivotal.io,

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.380
there's tons of information
about how you can configure

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.450
every part of your
deployment with BOSH if you'd

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:03.470
like more control.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:04.580
But if you're looking for
something a little bit

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.100
more hands off, we've got
Pivotal Cloud Foundry--

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.690
or PCF-- which comes
with Ops Manager.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.860
It's an appliance that
lives in your Google project

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.110
and can manage a lot of
this deployment for you.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:16.130
EVAN BROWN: Very cool.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.579
Thanks, everyone, for watching.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.940
Be sure to check out the
tutorials in the description.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.820
And stay tuned for the next
videos in this series where

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.400
other Pivotal and Google
engineers will talk about tools

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.430
they built including service
broker integrations, logging,

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.860
and monitoring for
BOSH and Cloud Foundry

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.640
on Google Cloud Platform.

